When one is unable to protect oneself and cannot be cured by tricks such as deception, and when faced with three powerful, ever-present enemies, if one asks, "What should be done?"—one should make the day not in vain, whether with a little or a lot of time. This means making the day fruitful (not empty) through the practice of Vipassanā (insight) meditation, even for a duration as short as the milking of a cow, or for the entire day and night. For every night this being discards, destroys, or spends, his life diminishes by that much. By this, it is shown that the passing of a night is indeed the passing of life, because that life cannot turn back. Therefore, it was said:

"The mortal first dwells in the womb for one night;
from the moment he is born, he only goes [towards death];
as he goes, he does not turn back."

One should consider the passing of life not only by the power of the night but also by the power of the postures. Thus, the verse beginning with "Carato" was said. "Carato" means for one who is walking. "Tiṭṭhato" means for one who is standing. "Āsīnasayanassa vā" means for one who is sitting or lying down. Some also read it as "Āsīdanaṃ"; in that case, it should be noted that the accusative case (upayogavacana) is used in the sense of possession (sāmī). "Upeti carimā rattī" means the night accompanied by the final consciousness reaches its end. Here, the mention of "night" is merely the pinnacle of the teaching (a representative expression). The final moment of a person engaged in any posture, such as walking, is indeed the moment of death. Those moments of posture pass only by exhausting the life-faculty (jīvitindriya). Therefore, it is not a time for you to be negligent; this is not the time for you to fall into negligence, because it is unknown that "death will not occur at such a time." Indeed, it was said:

"The life of mortals here is without sign and unknown;
it is difficult and brief, and joined with suffering."

Therefore, the meaning is that one should admonish oneself in this way and strive in the three trainings (sikkhā) with diligence.

## The Commentary on the Verses of Elder Sirimaṇḍa is finished.
